KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp26 --object-bits 12 --unwind 4 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ dot product: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
<linalg> ([linalg.algs.blas1.dot]): the dot product of (1, 2) with itself.  No
standard library implements it yet (libstdc++ 16, libc++ 18), so the test is
written to N5008 and not checked with a compiler; it is FUTURE until a library
provides the header.  Then it is a KNOWNBUG or CORE test of CBMC's support of
that library code.
